    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-45507-z",
    "abstract": "We use a unique combination of (paleo-)reanalysis (ModE-RA, ERA5) and model simulations (ModE-Sim) to examine extreme summer heat events in Central Europe over the past 600 years (1421-2008) and compare their occurrences to CMIP6 future climate projections. Using a common, fixed climatology, 2003 was identified as the hottest summer of the past 600 years. However, using a moving climatology approach, we identify 1540 (April\u2013September, +2.2 $$^\\circ$$C) and 1590 (June\u2013August, +2.8 $$^\\circ$$C) as the most extreme summer anomalies. The two summers differ in their temporal development, their impacts, and partly their atmospheric circulation patterns. We then analyze similarly anomalous summers in two sets of CMIP6 projections and the ModE-Sim ensemble and connect their occurrence to sea surface temperature anomalies over the North Atlantic. ModE-Sim, which comprises 11,760 model years, produces 0.14% (April-September) and 0.24% (June-August) Central European summers with temperature anomalies from a moving climatology surpassing 1540 and 1590. In CMIP6 projections such anomalies are more frequent, but none reaches the magnitude of the largest June-August anomaly in ModE-Sim which exceeds 4 $$^\\circ$$ $$^\\circ$$C. A similarly extreme anomaly in a future climate could dramatically affect ecosystems and societies. Overall, the combination of reanalysis and model simulations provides a unique and comprehensive framework for understanding the drivers of past and future summer heat extremes.",
    "field": "Paleoclimatology",
    "problem": "Assessing the rarity and drivers of past extreme summers is hindered by the lack of systematic, multi-variable, monthly-resolved climate data, which are necessary to understand their atmospheric processes and place modern and future heat events into a proper long-term context.",
    "method": "This study combines paleo-reanalysis, model simulations, and future projections to analyze 600 years of extreme Central European summers, using a moving climatology to compare temperature anomalies across different eras and identify the most severe events.",
    "task": "The task is to compare 600 years of historical extremely hot summers in Central Europe, using paleo-reanalysis and model simulations, with future climate projections to contextualize past extremes and assess their potential future occurrence.",
